<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cologne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cologne is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded as a Roman colony and an important centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>'s Rhine frontier. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its archbishop was one of the seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. It was also one of the largest and wealthiest cities of medieval Germany.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cologne.jpg" alt="Cologne" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Falbisoner · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kranh%C3%A4user_Cologne,_April_2018_-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cologne is centred on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, about 35 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> and 22 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, in the Cologne Lowland of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>. The city's highest point is 118 metres above sea level and its lowest 37.5 metres. It is regularly affected by flooding from the Rhine, and its flood control system was redesigned after a flood in 1993 that caused heavy damage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first urban settlement on the site was the Oppidum Ubiorum, founded in 38 BCE by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubii">Ubii</a>, a Germanic tribe. In 50 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonia-claudia-ara-agrippinensium">Colonia Claudia Ara Agrippinensium</a>, named after Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> and his wife Agrippina the Younger, who was born here. It became capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a> in 85, and from 260 to 271 was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-empire">Gallic Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/postumus">Postumus</a>, Marius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victorinus">Victorinus</a>. Emperor Constantine I had a bridge built over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at Cologne in 310. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> occupied the city in 462. Its population is put at about 40,000 in 100–200 CE, 20,000 in 1000 and 50,000 in 1200.</p><p>Cunibert, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Cologne in 623, advised the Merovingian King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagobert-i">Dagobert I</a>. In 795 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s bishop Hildebold was promoted to archbishop, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> of 843 placed the city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a>'s Middle Francia.</p><p>Its position on major trade routes between eastern and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> made it a free imperial city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and a major member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, and one of the largest European cities of the medieval and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> periods. French forces occupied it in 1794, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> of 1801 made the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> part of the French Republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>, introduced under French rule, remained in use there until 1900. In 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave Cologne to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cologne is the fourth-most populous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, with over one million inhabitants. It was one of the most heavily bombed cities in Germany during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, which reduced its population by 93%, mainly through evacuation, and destroyed around 80% of the old city centre. Most major historic landmarks were restored, among them Cologne Cathedral, built to house the Shrine of the Three Kings and the world's tallest building from 1880 to 1890, and the Romanesque churches. Eau de Cologne has been produced in the city since 1709.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cologne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 496, traditionally at Tolbiac (Zülpich) near <b>Cologne</b>, the Alemanni fought Clovis I; Gregory of Tours tells how Clovis, close to defeat, vowed to become a Christian if he won, and how the Alemanni submitted once their king had been killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Wittelsbachs repeated partitions weakened the duchy for two and a half centuries, until the Landshut War of Succession ended with the <b>Cologne</b> arbitration of 1505, which reunited most of the lands under Albert IV, though the Palatinate-Neuburg was carved out for his rivals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…came under John I, who in the War of the Limburg Succession defeated Siegfried of Westerburg, archbishop of <b>Cologne</b>, and Count Reinald I of Guelders at the Battle of Worringen on 5 June 1288, and so acquired the Duchy of Limburg and control of the trade route from…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Henry was condemned, and in 1180 the Gelnhausen charter divided Saxony: the western part became the Duchy of Westphalia, granted to the archbishop of <b>Cologne</b>, and the ducal title with the eastern lands went to Bernhard of Anhalt, a son of Albert the Bear of the Ascanian family.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1672 France, England, Münster and <b>Cologne</b> attacked together, and the Republic survived by flooding the Holland Water Line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In Lotharingia it was held in the eleventh century by the Ezzonid family, with lands around Aachen and <b>Cologne</b>, but pressure from the archbishops of Cologne pushed the counts palatine southwards to the middle Rhine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
